{"page_content": "spread and material flammability.  That follow-on plan limits the duration of ACME operations, \nwhere each ACME experiment is allotted four months for testing.  Although it requires additional \ncrew time, these allotments are split into two rounds so that the investigators have a break from \ntesting for careful review of the early results so that they can maximize the scientific outcome of \ntheir second round.  This paper reports on early results from the first round of E-FIELD Flames. \n \nWhile nitrogen is available from the space station, the combustion testing is limited by the \navailable gas bottles that were and are launched to support the tests.  For E-FIELD Flames and \nmost of ACME\u2019s experiments, the test matrices used to plan those gas resources were established \nin 2010.  At that time, NASA limited the scope of the ACME tests matrices with the intent of \nfocused experiments with limited objectives rather than comprehensive experiments.  As such, \nthe general target was 50 tests per experiment.  For most ACME experiments, the limiting gas \nconstraint is the oxygen/nitrogen bottles used to fill the chamber.  This resource constraint is \nexacerbated by the CIR because it is equipped with a pair of redundant safety timers to limit the \nfuel that can flow into the combustion chamber (where the timers can be set for the oxidizer flow \nin the case of inverse flame testing).  The timers can only be reset by venting the combustion \nchamber.  Therefore, the timer limit combined with other constraints effectively corresponds to \nthe day\u2019s maximum flow time for the fuel (or more generally, the limiting reactant).  For E-FIELD \nFlames, the safety timers are set to the maximum value of 999 seconds, which corresponds to the \n10 tests typically completed per day.", "metadata": {"source": "PSI-10_Reports_FinalRpt_ACMEFlameDesign.pdf", "page": 22}}